449    /** FUNCTION otap_test.match_regex
450    * Checks given data of type VARCHAR2 against an Oracle REGEX expression. Uses REGEXP_LIKE.
451    * ATTENTION Oracle REGEX implementation is not standard. Unix regex which work like charm take hours to implement in
452    * Oracle REGEX to work as desired. Test your expression well with Oracle before using it.
453    *
454    * Easiest way to check is SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dual WHERE regexp_like('your string', 'your regex', 'regex param');
455    * Should result in 1 if successful checked. You may want to prepare a with block with different string to pass them
456    * through the regular expression.
457    *
458    * @param p_have The data to check.
459    * @param p_regex A valid Oracle regular expression that p_have must match.
460    * @param p_description The test description if any. If not given, a description is generated, see template.
461    * @param p_param Parameter for REGEXP_LIKE. 'i' is case insensitive. See Oracle documentation for details, https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/21/sqlrf/Pattern-matching-Conditions.html#GUID-D2124F3A-C6E4-4CCA-A40E-2FFCABFD8E19.
462    * @param p_expected_result The expected test result as number. Default is test passed. See otap_constants.
463    *
464    * @return The test result as text.
465    */
466    FUNCTION match_regex( p_have	       IN     VARCHAR2
467  			   , p_regex	       IN     VARCHAR2
468  			   , p_description     IN     VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
469  			   , p_param	       IN     VARCHAR2 DEFAULT NULL
470  			   , p_expected_result IN     NUMBER   DEFAULT otap_constants.OTAP_NUM_TEST_PASSED
471  			   )
472  	 RETURN VARCHAR2
473    ;
